          %h3#1CatIntro For 1-category theorists
          %p
            This section assumes that the reader is well familiar with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
            and maybe with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+omega-categories"} strict omega-categories
            but in need of some introductory words on
            = succeed "-categories." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,n)
          %p
            Ordinary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
            provides various powerful tools for generating higher order structures, among them notably
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+category+theory"} enrichment
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internalization"}> internalization
                \.
          %p
            Here we are interested in higher order
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categories"} categories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cat"} Cat
            itself as a 1-categorical context for either of these procedures. Since
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cat"} Cat
            naturally a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+monoidal+category"} cartesian monoidal category
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝒱
                  %mo ,
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≔
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Cat
                  %mo ,
                  %mo ×
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  (\mathcal{V}, \otimes) \coloneqq (Cat, \times)
          %p
            we may form the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+V-enriched+categories"} category of V-enriched categories
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒱
                    %mi Cat
                    %mo ≔
                    %mi Cat
                    %mi Cat
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{V}Cat \coloneqq Cat Cat
            = succeed "-category" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Cat
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                a collection of
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/objects"} objects
            %li
              %p
                for each pair of objects
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi A
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
                a
                %em category
                of morphisms, hence to be thought of as collection of ordinary morphisms
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo →
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \to B
                together with
                = succeed ":" do
                  %em morphisms between these morphisms
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-morphisms"} 2-morphisms
            %li
              %p
                such that composition is a
                %em functor
                on these hom-categories.
          %p
            This is the structure of a
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+2-category"} strict 2-category
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Cat
                  %mi Cat
                  %mo ≃
                  %mi Str
                  %mn 2
                  %mi Cat
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  Cat Cat \simeq Str 2 Cat
                  \\,.
          %p is the category of strict 2-categories.
          %p
            By general results of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+category+theory"} enriched category theory
            (or by immediate inspection), this is still a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+monoidal+category"} cartesian monoidal category
            and so we may iterate this and consider now the enriching category
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝒱
                  %mo ,
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≔
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Cat
                  %mi Cat
                  %mo ,
                  %mo ×
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  (\mathcal{V}, \otimes) \coloneqq (Cat Cat, \times)
          %p
            and construct again
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒱
                    %mi Cat
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{V}Cat
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Cat
                  %mi Cat
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi Cat
                  %mo ≃
                  %mi Str
                  %mn 3
                  %mi Cat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  (Cat Cat) Cat \simeq Str 3 Cat
          %p
            the category of strict
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/3-categories"} 3-categories
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi ℕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \in \mathbb{N}
            the
            = succeed "-fold" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Cat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat
            is the category
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Str
                  %mi n
                  %mi Cat
                  %mo ≃
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Cat
                  %mi Cat
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi Cat
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi Cat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  Str n Cat \simeq  (\cdots ((Cat Cat)Cat) \cdots) Cat
          %p
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+n-categories"} strict n-categories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inductive+limit"} inductive limit
            of this construction finally is the category of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+omega-categories"} strict omega-categories
          %p
            While this easily generates
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+category+theory"} higher categorical structures
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-category"} 2-category
            still happens to be
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+2-categories"} equivalent
            to a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+2-category"} strict 2-category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/3-category"} 3-category
            is no longer equivalent to a strict 3-category, and the discrepancy only increases with
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
          %p
            But inspection in the case of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-categories"} 2-categories
            already shows what the problem is: in a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bicategory"} weak 2-category
            structural relations such as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associativity"} associativity
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unitality"} unitality
            no longer hold as equations but only
            %em up to
            an
            %em invertible
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-morphism"} 2-morphism
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Str
                    %mn 2
                    %mi Cat
                    %mo ≃
                    %mi Cat
                    %mi Cat
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Str 2 Cat \simeq Cat Cat
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+category"} enriched category
            %em strictly
            – therefore the name.
          %p
            But this problem directly corresponds to an evident shortcoming of the very starting point of the above recursive construction: that construction regarded
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cat"} Cat
            as a 1-category in order to fit it into the standard formulation of
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+category+theory"} enriched category theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cat"} Cat
            is naturally rather a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-category"} 2-category
            itself. The enrichment procedure should be allowed to make use of this extra structure. On the other hand, as we have just seen, the failure of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Cat
                  %mi Cat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat Cat
            to model all of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2Cat"} 2Cat
            is only in the lack of
            %em invertible
            2-morphisms. Therefore what should really matter for the improved enrichment is just the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%282%2C1%29-category"} (2,1)-category
            underlying
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cat"} Cat
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categories"} categories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functors"} functors
            between them, but only
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+isomorphism"} natural isomorphism
            instead of all
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+transformations"} natural transformations
            between those.
          %p
            This way one does arrive at a suitable refined notion of enrichment over the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%282%2C1%29-category"} (2,1)-category
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Cat
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Cat
                  %mi Cat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat Cat
            then indeed produces all of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2Cat"} 2Cat
          %p
            However, this only fixed the first step of the above recursive definition. In the next step we want
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 2
                  %mi Cat
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi Cat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (2 Cat)Cat
            to produce all
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/3-categories"} 3-categories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coherence"} coherence
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/3-morphisms"} 3-morphisms
            which do not appear in enriched
            = succeed "-category" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 2
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (2,1)
          %p
            This shows that the natural starting point for a construction of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-categories"} n-categories
            by recursive enrichment must be a conception of 1-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
            which knows already about
            %em invertible
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/k-morphisms"} k-morphisms
            for all
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            %em invertible
            higher morphisms is that of
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} (∞,1)-category
            = succeed "-categories" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
          %p
            If we then just replace in the above the naive
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cat"} Cat
            with
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29Cat"} (∞,1)Cat
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Cat
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn ∞
                      %mo ,
                      %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:lspace => "mediummathspace", :rspace => "mediummathspace"} ∶−
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi Cat
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn ∞
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %msub
                    %mi Cat
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn ∞
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %msub
                    %mi Cat
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn ∞
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %msub
                    %mi Cat
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn ∞
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  Cat_{(\infty,n)} \coloneq (\cdots ((Cat_{(\infty,1)} Cat_{(\infty,1)})Cat_{(\infty,1)}) \cdots) Cat_{(\infty,1)}
          %p
            does produce a good general notion of
            = succeed "-categories," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %em
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,n)
              \-categories
            discussed here.
          %p
            There is also an alternative road to the same conclusion: another standard procedure for producing higher order structures from the 1-category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cat"} Cat
            is to consider
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+categories"} internal categories
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Cat
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            a category with
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+limits"} finite limits
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Cat
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi E
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat(E)
            for the category of
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+categories"} internal categories
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Cat
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Cat
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat(Cat)
            for the category of
            = succeed "-internal" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Cat
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat
          %p
            This gives
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/double+categories"} double categories
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi DoubleCat
                  %mo ≃
                  %mi Cat
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Cat
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  DoubleCat \simeq Cat(Cat)
          %p
            and hence again not quite the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-categories"} 2-categories
            that we are after. But it is of interest to note that now there are
            %em two
            problems, not just the one above: while a
            = succeed "-internal" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Cat
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associativity"} associativity
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unitality"} unitality
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+2-category"} strict 2-category
          %p
            The first problem turns out to be solved as before: instead of working with the 1-category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cat"} Cat
            we should already regard that as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%282%2C1%29-category"} (2,1)-category
            and then formulate
            %em internal (2,1)-categories
            in straightforward generalization of the ordinary notion. For the second problem it turns out that one needs to slightly enhance that straightforward generalization and add a condition known (somewhat undescriptively) as
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complete+Segal+space"} completeness
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+category+in+an+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} internal category in an (∞,1)-category
            = succeed "-categories" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,n)
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Cat
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn ∞
                      %mo ,
                      %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≃
                  %mi Cat
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Cat
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi Cat
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn ∞
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  Cat_{(\infty,n)} \simeq Cat(\cdots(Cat(Cat_{(\infty,0)}))\cdots)
                  \\,.
          %h3#HomotopyIntro For homotopy theorists
          %p
            This section assumes that the reader is well familiar with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
            and maybe with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category+theory"} (∞,1)-category theory
            but in need of some introductory words on
            = succeed "-categories." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,n)
          %p
            A fundamental insight of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
            is, of course, that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+shapes+for+higher+structures"} cellular shape
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplices"} simplices
            naturally serves to model paths and higher
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopies"} homotopies
            in “spaces”, which here really means: in
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+types"} homotopy types
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-groupoids"} ∞-groupoids
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Δ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta[n]
            is naturally identified with the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/total+order"} linear category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mn 0
                  %mo →
                  %mn 1
                  %mo →
                  %mn 2
                  %mo →
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mo →
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{0 \to 1 \to 2 \to \cdots \to n\}
            on
            = succeed "-objects," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo +
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n+1)
            %em direction
            on the paths which form the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+skeleton"} 1-skeleton
            of a map
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Δ
                      %mi n
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta^n \to X
          %p
            If
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is a
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+set"} simplicial set
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type"} homotopy type
          %p
            But it is straightforward to consider a slight generalization of this situation where we take
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            to be such that
            %em not
            all paths in it have inverses. Still thinking of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            as a homotopy type this may be thought of as modelling a
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/directed+homotopy+theory"} directed homotopy type
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            instead modeled as a simplicial set, this has been formalized by the concept of a
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasi-category"} quasi-category
            or
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} (∞,1)-category
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/directed+homotopy+theory"} directed homotopy types
            in direct generalization of how
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kan+complexes"} Kan complexes
            are combinatorial models for ordinary
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+types"} homotopy types
          %p
            As the notation already suggests, the idea of
            = succeed "-category" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,n)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-groupoids"} ∞-groupoids
            (“(
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn ∞
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty,0
            )-categories”) to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-categories"} (∞,1)-categories
            is but the first step in a tower of higher generalizations, where in step
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            one considers “directed homotopy” up to and including dimension
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
          %p
            It is natural that such
            %em
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,n)
              \-categories
            should be probed by corresponding higher dimensional analogs of the objects in the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplex+category"} simplex category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Δ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mn 0
                  %mo →
                  %mn 1
                  %mo →
                  %mn 2
                  %mo →
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mo →
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta[n] = \{0 \to 1 \to 2 \to \cdots \to n\}
            that support traditional homotopy theory. There are many such generalizations which one could consider. One which has proven to be useful are the objects in the
            = succeed "th" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Theta-category"} Theta-category
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Θ
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Theta_n
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pasting"} pasting
            – of cellular intervals, the objects of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Θ
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Theta_n
            arise from
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pasting"} pasting
            of
            = succeed "-dimensional" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/globes"} globes
            (an interval being a 1-dimensional globe).
          %p
            Accordingly, just as an
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-groupoid"} ∞-groupoid
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type"} homotopy type
            may be presented by a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+set"} simplicial set
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presheaf"} presheaf
            on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplex+category"} simplex category
            – or more generally by a
            = succeed "\u2013" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bisimplicial+set"} simplicial space
            = succeed "-)condition" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kan+complex"} Kan filler
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2Cn%29-category"} (∞,n)-category
            may be presented by a presheaf of spaces on the
            = succeed "th" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Theta-category"} Theta-category
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
          %h2#Definition Definitions
          %p
            There are various different ways of defining
            = succeed "-categories," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,n)
          %p
            There is an axiomatic characterization of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} (∞,1)-category
            of
            = succeed "-categories" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,n)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generators+and+relations"} generation
            from
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+n-categories"} strict n-categories
          %ul
            %li
              %a{:href => "#AxiomaticCharacterization"} Definition via generation from strict n-categories
          %p
            Among the more direct definitions of
            = succeed "-categories" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,n)
            = succeed "-categories" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,n)
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+category"} enrichment
            over
            = succeed "-categories" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
